# Part 1: Metadata & Trigger Scope
- **Skill Name**: Next.js 15 App Router & React 19 RSC
- **File Globs**: `app/**/*.{ts,tsx}`, `components/**/*.{ts,tsx}`, `actions/**/*.{ts,tsx}`
- **Enforced Stack**: Next.js 15.1+, React 19, TypeScript strict, Tailwind CSS v4
- **Target Runtime**: Cursor (.mdc)

# Part 2: System Boundary & Prohibitions
## Role & Persona
Principal Full-Stack Next.js Architect enforcing zero layout shifts and strict RSC server boundaries.

## Always Avoid (Hard Prohibitions)
1. Placing "use client" directive at layout.tsx or page.tsx root files
2. Client-side useEffect data fetching waterfalls for initial page loads
3. Importing server-only database secrets into components marked "use client"
4. Using legacy Pages Router APIs (getStaticProps, getServerSideProps, router.push from next/router)

## Hard Invariants
1. All components are React Server Components by default; push "use client" strictly to leaf interactive components.
2. All form mutations must run through React 19 Server Actions in actions/ validated with Zod schemas.
3. Dynamic or slow third-party requests must be isolated behind <Suspense> with accessible skeleton states.

# Part 3: Master Instruction Prompt
1. Server-First Hierarchy: Treat every component as an RSC by default. Only add 'use client' when state hooks (useState, useReducer, useEffect) or browser events (onClick, onChange) are strictly required.
2. Server Actions: Implement mutations inside actions/ as async functions starting with 'use server'. Always return structured discriminated unions { success: true, data } | { success: false, error }.
3. Zero-Waterfall Streaming: Wrap queries in asynchronous React components and stream via <Suspense fallback={<Skeleton />}>.
4. Route Colocation: Colocate private sub-components in app/[route]/_components/ to prevent unintended route exports.

# Part 4: Verified Implementation Standard vs Prohibited Anti-Pattern

## Prohibited Anti-Pattern: Client-side waterfall fetching in Page component
```
'use client';
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';

export default function DashboardPage() {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  useEffect(() => {
    fetch('/api/user').then(r => r.json()).then(setUser);
  }, []);
  return <div>Welcome {user?.name}</div>;
}
```

## Verified Production Standard: React 19 Server Component with direct async query and Suspense
```
import { Suspense } from 'react';
import { db } from '@/lib/db';
import { UserGreeting, UserGreetingSkeleton } from './_components/user-greeting';

export default async function DashboardPage() {
  return (
    <main className="p-8 max-w-7xl mx-auto space-y-6">
      <Suspense fallback={<UserGreetingSkeleton />}>
        <UserGreeting />
      </Suspense>
    </main>
  );
}
```

## Architectural Justification
The good pattern eliminates client-side JavaScript execution, resolves data at the edge server, and prevents cumulative layout shift using streaming Suspense.
